<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">King James Version</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1611">1611</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as King James Bible, Authorized Version</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The King James Version is the English translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> commissioned by James I and published in 1611 for use in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. It was prepared by around fifty scholars working in committees, drawing heavily on William Tyndale's earlier translation. Its language shaped English prose and literature for centuries, and it remained the standard English Bible for most Protestants until the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-james-version.jpg" alt="King James Version" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Church of England · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KJV-King-James-Version-Bible-first-edition-title-page-1611.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The King James Version was the third translation into English approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, after the Great Bible of 1535 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>' Bible of 1568. The Geneva Bible, produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1560, proved more popular with the laity, but its footnotes represented a Calvinistic Puritanism. In 1525 William Tyndale began translating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, the first printed Bible in English. He was executed for heresy, but his work became the ultimate basis for all later renditions into Early Modern English.</p><p>King James convened the Hampton Court Conference in January 1604, and it proposed a new version in response to problems the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> perceived in the earlier ones. The translators were told to follow the ecclesiology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> and to avoid marginal notes where possible, since the Geneva Bible's notes had been criticised as Puritan and anti-monarchical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The translators were all members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and all but Sir Henry Savile were clergy. They worked in six committees, two each at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and Westminster, and included scholars with Puritan sympathies as well as high churchmen. Forty unbound copies of the 1602 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>' Bible were printed so that each committee could record its changes in the margins. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> the translators used the Textus Receptus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> was revised from the Bishops' Bible with reference to the Masoretic Text, and the Apocrypha was rendered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> and the Latin Vulgate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Robert Barker, the King's Printer, published the first edition in 1611 as a complete folio, sold looseleaf for ten shillings or bound for twelve. Barker fell into debt and sub-leased his privilege to Bonham Norton and John Bill, and bitter financial disputes followed. No record of the authorisation survives, because the Privy Council registers for 1600 to 1613 were destroyed by fire. The version replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>' Bible as the lectern Bible in parish churches, and in the 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> it supplanted the Great Bible in the Epistle and Gospel readings.</p><p>By the first half of the 18th century it was effectively unchallenged in Anglican and other English Protestant churches. Misprints and varying modernised texts became a scandal by the mid-18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> standard text of 1769 became the basis of almost all later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a>, often without the Apocrypha. With stereotype printing in the early 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> became the most widely printed book in history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King James Version” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Elizabethan and Jacobean age produced the drama of William Shakespeare, Christopher Marlowe and Ben Jonson, and the <b>King James Bible</b> of 1611 shaped English prose for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Coptic; Jerome’s Latin Vulgate became the Bible of the medieval West; the missionaries Cyril and Methodius created a Slavonic script in the 9th century to translate the scriptures; and the Reformation produced vernacular Bibles such as Luther’s German Bible and the English <b>King James Version</b> of 1611.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Mormons believe in the Old and New Testaments, and the LDS Church uses the Authorized <b>King James Version</b> as its official Bible text.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mormonism">Mormonism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Luther’s Bible helped to form standard written German, and translations such as the Welsh Bible of 1588 and the <b>King James Version</b> of 1611 became classics of their languages.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The 16th century brought an explosion of vernacular translations: Martin Luther's (1522), William Tyndale's (1526) and the <b>Authorized Version</b>, or King James Version (1611).</blockquote><a href="/work/new-testament">New Testament · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reformation spread vernacular Bibles, among them the Geneva Bible (1557) and the <b>King James Bible</b> (1611), whose wording became the best known English form of the psalm.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li></ul></section>
